    <h1>Exercise Name: Seated Dumbbell Shoulder Press</h1>
    <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>
The video demonstrates the Seated Dumbbell Shoulder Press (also known as the Seated Dumbbell Overhead Press). It is a fundamental compound exercise that primarily targets the shoulders while engaging your upper body stability.
 Primary: Anterior (front) and lateral (side) deltoids.
 Secondary: Triceps, upper pectorals (chest), and upper trapezius.

</div></div>
<pre>

<h1>Step-by-Step Execution Guide</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>:
 Adjust an adjustable bench to an upright position (around 80°–90° angle).
 Sit firmly with your back pressed against the backrest and your feet planted flat on the floor for stability.
 Pick up a dumbbell in each hand and lift them to shoulder height using your thighs for assistance if necessary.
 Rotate your wrists so your palms face forward (pronated grip) with elbows bent at roughly a 90-degree angle.


<u><b><h4>2. The Press (Concentric Phase)</h4></b></u>:
 Exhale and push the dumbbells overhead in a smooth, controlled arc until your arms are fully extended directly above your shoulders.
 Avoid slamming the dumbbells together at the top or locking out your elbows aggressively.


<u><b><h4>3. The Return (Eccentric Phase)</h4></b></u>:
 Inhale as you lower the dumbbells slowly back down to ear/shoulder level.
 Maintain tension in your shoulders throughout the motion without letting the weights drop suddenly.





<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Shoulder Hypertrophy & Strength:</b> Highly effective for building cap-like deltoids and overall upper-body press strength.
 <b>◉Balanced Muscle Development:</b> Using independent dumbbells prevents your dominant side from overcompensating, correcting strength imbalances between shoulders.
 <b>◉Core & Back Support:</b> The seated backrest stabilizes your lower back, allowing you to focus purely on pressing heavier loads with reduced spinal strain.
 <b>◉Joint Comfort:</b> Dumbbells allow your wrists and elbows to follow a natural path of motion, making it easier on the shoulder joints compared to a fixed barbell.


<h1>Sets & Repetition Recommendations</h1>

 Muscle Building (Hypertrophy): 3–4 sets of 8–12 reps with moderate-to-heavy weight.
 Strength Focus: 3–5 sets of 5–8 reps with heavier weight.
 Endurance & Toning: 3 sets of 12–15 reps with lighter weight.
 Rest: 60–90 seconds between sets (or 2 minutes for heavy strength sets).
